UID:c4d08de87d3d7a8e@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261013T224313Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261013
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Labor Department finds 6 New York\, New Je
 rsey construction employers exposed workers to safety hazards\, assesses c
 ombined $648K in penalties
DESCRIPTION:MONROE\, NY – The U.S. Department of Labor has cited six con
 struction employers for willful and serious safety violations involving ex
 cavation hazards at the site of a planned 408\,000 square-foot mixed-use c
 ommercial building and proposed a combined $648\,094 in penalties. The de
 partment’s Occupational Safety and Health Administration responded to an
  allegation of struck-by and collapse hazards associated with a large exca
 vation at the Monroe Commons project – also known as Magna Square develo
 pment – a planned 4-story\, approximately 408\,000 square-foot mixed-use
  commercial building with retail\, office space\, and a 39-room hotel loca
 ted on an 18-acre site. The excavation had a steep\, unstable embankment n
 early 75 feet high which was not adequately sloped or shored. OSHA issued
  a total of 23 serious and five willful citations to the project’s gener
 al contractor and five subcontractors for failing to protect employees fro
 m cave-in\, struck-by\, and chemical hazards\; neglecting to shield employ
 ees from falling or rolling rock and soil from an excavation face\; and no
 t ensuring daily inspections of excavations were conducted by a competent 
 person. Advanced Plan and Build LLC\, and subcontractors Parkstone Inc. a
 nd Obstacle Systems Inc. – operating as Drip Drop Waterproofing Inc. –
  and The Paving Masters LLC received citations for willfully exposing empl
 oyees to cave-in and struck-by hazards from an unprotected excavation. OSH
 A also cited Advanced Plan and Build and The Paving Masters for their will
 ful failure to ensure that the excavation\, which was between 21 and 75 fe
 et deep\, was designed by a registered professional engineer. Subcontract
 ors Century Max Industries Corp.\, DelValle Contractor\, and Prime Structu
 re Inc. were cited for serious violations for failing to protect employees
  from cave-in and struck-by hazards. DelValle and Parkstone Inc. and Obsta
 cle Systems Inc. also received citations for serious violations related to
  lack of fall protection and chemical hazards.OSH\nhttp://www.dol.gov/news
 room/releases/osha/osha20261013
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osha/osha20261013
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cfb2ab8242bc914c@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261013T224217Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261013
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Federally Required MaineCare Off-Cycle Provid
 er Revalidation Plan: October 2026 Update
DESCRIPTION:Maine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Ma
 ine.gov\nDHHS\n→\nOffice of MaineCare Services (OMS)\n→\nProvider Reso
 urces\n→\n	 \n			Federally Required MaineCare Off-Cycle Provider Revalid
 ation Plan: October 2026 Update\nFederally Required MaineCare Off-Cycle Pr
 ovider Revalidation Plan: October 2026 Update\nOct 13\, 2026\nAs\nprevious
 ly communicated\n\, the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) r
 equested all states develop and submit a comprehensive two-year provider r
 evalidation strategy\, with priority given to revalidating high-risk provi
 ders.\nYou can find Maine’s two-year plan and a list of all providers i
 ncluded in the off-cycle revalidation on the Office of MaineCare Services 
 (OMS)\nFederally Required 2026-2028 Off-Cycle Revalidation Plan webpage\n.
  OMS grouped providers into five cohorts and sends notices alerting provid
 ers at least 30 days prior to initiating the revalidation window for their
  assigned cohort. OMS additionally sends a 60-day post-initiation notice\,
  per standard revalidation practices.\nMilestones to note:\nCohorts 1 & 2:
  Individual high-risk providers\, providers without an NPI\, Personal Care
  Agencies\, Home Health Agencies\, Hospice\, and Assisted Living Services 
 providers\nOn July 19\, 2026\, OMS mailed a 30-day notice to impacted prov
 iders in Cohorts 1 and 2 announcing that their revalidation window would o
 pen on August 17\, 2026.\nProviders in Cohorts 1 and 2 who do not submit a
  revalidation case by\nFriday October 16\, 2026\n\, will have claims pend 
 until they submit their revalidation case.\nProviders who miss the October
  16th deadline will have until November 16\, 2026\, to submit their revali
 dation case. Any provider who has not submitted a required revalidation ca
 se by that date will be terminated effective November 17\, 2026.\nYou can 
 find more information about the provider revalidation process on our\nProv
 ider Enrollment and Revalidation webpage\n.\nQuestions?\nFor questions abo
 ut this off-cycle revalidation process\, please email\nKenneth Jamison\,\n
 Provide\nhttps://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/feder
 ally-required-mainecare-cycle-provider-revalidation-plan-october-2026-upda
 te-2026-10
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/federally-
 required-mainecare-cycle-provider-revalidation-plan-october-2026-update-20
 26-10
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
