DTSTAMP:20261006T000145Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261005
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine CDC Encourages Tick Bite Prevention Thi
 s Fall
DESCRIPTION:Oct 05\, 2026\nReminds Maine people and visitors ticks remain 
 active even as temperatures fall\nAs days get shorter and leaves begin to 
 change\, ticks remain active across Maine. The Maine Department of Health 
 and Human Services’ Center for Disease Control and Prevention (Maine CDC
 ) urges all Maine people and visitors to protect themselves from tick bite
 s this fall.\nTicks can be active throughout the fall and during winter da
 ys\, whenever temperatures rise above freezing. Fall is a busy time for ou
 tdoor activities\, bringing people into contact with ticks more often. Peo
 ple may encounter them during everyday fall activities\, like cleaning the
  yard or hunting.\nCurrent year preliminary counts from the\nMaine Trackin
 g Network\nshow that health care providers reported over 5\,000 cases of t
 ickborne disease so far this year. This includes diseases like Lyme diseas
 e\, anaplasmosis\, and babesiosis.\n“Many people think ticks are only a 
 summer problem\, but ticks can remain active throughout the fall\,”\nsai
 d Maine CDC Director Puthiery Va.\n“You can enjoy Maine’s beautiful fa
 ll weather and outdoor activities while staying safe by taking a few simpl
 e steps before\, during\, and after can help protect you from tickborne di
 sease.”\nTicks often live in wooded and brushy areas and areas with leaf
  litter. The Maine CDC encourages everyone to think TICKS when spending ti
 me outdoors:\nT – Take an EPA-approved repellent\nand use it before goin
 g outside. Use DEET\, picaridin\, IR3535\, or oil of lemon eucalyptus on s
 kin. Pre-treat clothing with permethrin.\nI – Inspect your body daily.\n
 Check yourself\, children\, and pets for ticks after spending time outdoor
 s.\nC – Cover your skin.\nWear long-sleeved shirts and long pants and tu
 ck pants into socks. Light-colored clothing makes ticks easier to spot.\nK
  – Know your surroundings.\nBe aware when you are in tick habitat and ta
 ke extra precautions.\nS – Shower after being outdoors.\nShowering helps
  wash off ticks before they attach. Put clothes in the dryer on high heat\
 nhttps://www.maine.gov/dhhs/news/maine-cdc-encourages-tick-bite-prevention
 -fall-2026-10-05
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/news/maine-cdc-encourages-tick-bite-prevent
 ion-fall-2026-10-05
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
