UID:13b2054f1ad32ed0@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261001T223937Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine Paid Family and Medical Leave Program P
 ublishes Actuarial Report\, First Claims Report
DESCRIPTION:Skip First Level Navigation\nSkip All Navigation\nMaine.gov\nA
 gencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nMaine Departmen
 t of Labor\nContact Us\n|\nNews\n|\nOnline services\n|\nPublications\n|\nS
 itemap\nHome\nContact Us\nOnline Services\nAbout MDOL\nNews & Events\nForm
 s & Publications\nMDOL Bureaus\nHome\n→\nNews & Events\n→ Maine Paid F
 amily and Medical Leave Program Publishes Actuarial Report\, First Claims 
 Report\nServices\nJobs & Training\nUnemployment Insurance\nDisability Serv
 ices\nWorkplace Safety\nLabor Laws\nLabor Statistics\nWorkforce Developmen
 t\nMaine Paid Family and Medical Leave Program Publishes Actuarial Report\
 , First Claims Report\nOctober 1\, 2026\nFor Immediate Release: Oct. 1\, 2
 026\nMedia Contact: Autumn Mahoney\, Communications Specialist media.dol@m
 aine.gov\nActuarial report projects PFML Trust Fund will remain stable for
  the next 10 years\nAUGUSTA - The Maine Paid Family and Medical Leave (PFM
 L) program has published its second annual\nactuarial report\n\, as well a
 s its first\nclaims report\non Oct. 1\, 2026.\nPFML became law in 2023 and
  is a statewide program that provides eligible workers with up to 12 weeks
  of paid leave for medical\, parental\, family care\, military family or s
 afe leave.\nThe PFML program began accepting claims on March 30\, 2026\, a
 nd began paying benefits on May 1\, 2026. This initial claims report cover
 s the program's first three months of claims activity\, from March 30 thro
 ugh June 30\, 2026. Future claims reports will be published annually and w
 ill cover a full year of claims activity.\nHighlights of the reports inclu
 de:\nThe PFML Trust Fund remains solvent and based on updated revenue and 
 claim expenditure\, is projected to remain stable for the next 10 years.\n
 The program received 8\,376 applications between March 30 and June 30\, 20
 26.\nAmong the claims approved\, medical leave was the most common reason\
 , followed by welcoming a new child and caring for a family member. Other 
 leave reasons represented a very small portion of the overall claim volume
